                    Archive and Compression

Archive:
--> Archiving means combining multiple files and directories into single file.
--> It is used to organize data effciently, especially when dealing 
with backups or data transfer.

tar command :
--> It is short for Tape Archive.
--> It is used to create or extract archive file.
syntax:

tar <options> <archive_file_name> <file or dir to be archived>

options:
-c : create archive file
-f : file name
-v : verbose mode
-t : list the contents of archived file
-x : extract
-C : extract contents to another location.

tar -cvf newetc.tar /etc
--> to create archive file

tar -tvf newetc.tar
--> to list out contents of newetc.tar

tar -xvf newetc.tar
--> to extract file

tar -xvf newetc.tar -C /mnt
--> to extact at /mnt

du -sh : (Disk Usage) 
here,
-s = Summery
-h = Human Readable
---> used to display file size
syntax: du -sh <file_name>
eg: du -sh /etc

                         **compression**

--> Compresssion reduces the size of the file to save disk space and speed up
data transfer.
--> Tools for compression:
1. gzip
2. bzip2
3. xz

1. gzip : Compression
syntax: gzip <file_name>
i/p: gzip new.tar
o/p: new.tar.gz  

--> gunzip : decompression
syntax: gunzip <file_name>
i/p : gunzip new.tar.gz
o/p: new.tar

2. bzip2:
syntax: bzip2 <file_name>
i/p: bzip2 new.tar
o/p: new.tar.bz2 

--> bunzip2:
syntax: bunzip2 <file_name>
i/p: bunzip2 new.tar.bz2
o/p: new.tar 

3. xz:
syntax: xz <file_name>
i/p: xz new.tar
o/p: new.tar.xz

--> unxz:
syntax: unxz <file_name>
i/p: unxz new.tar.xz
o/p: new.tar

**use compression tools with tar command:

gzip:
option: -z
extension: .gz 

bzip:
option: -j
extension: .bz2

xz:
option: -J 
extension: .xz


tar -czvf new.tar.gz /etc 
--> new.tar.gz  





